SIGSEGVs under BIT_GC=stress against a Bit-sourced libbitrt.a. Tracked as #1835,
which must remove this file when it closes.

Why this program and no other: rootbit tests the object model BELOW the type
system, so every intermediate it holds is a raw `int` address rather than a typed
reference, and several live in module-level `[]i64` storage that is untraced by
definition. Nothing in the root set enumerates "addresses a program stashed
somewhere the tracer was told not to look", and nothing should.

What the waiver costs, stated plainly rather than implied: nothing. Until G3 this
program allocated from a private heap the live runtime collector had never been told
about, so not one of its objects was ever a collection candidate and every
assertion it has ever made was made with the collector effectively off. Sharing
the live heap is what first subjected it to real collection, and it did not
survive. BIT_GC=off is therefore not a downgrade from coverage that existed - it
is the coverage this program has always had, now labelled.

Do not close #1835 by giving rootbit its private heap back. That restores the
inertness above and hides whichever of the two answers is true: either a real
root-set hole in the shipped runtime, or a program that cannot hold raw addresses
across a collection and must say so. Seven candidate causes are eliminated on the
ticket; the next step there is a measurement, not a fix.
